{%- comment -%}
  Default FAQs per template. Used when the template has no FAQ blocks of its own
  (tile FAQ blocks are skipped on non-tile templates). Add FAQ blocks in the theme editor to override.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- case cat -%}
  {%- when 'tiles' -%}
    {%- liquid
      assign fq = product.metafields.custom
      assign fq_s = fq.tile_surface.value | default: fq.floor_or_wall.value | append: '' | downcase
      assign fq_slip = fq.slip_rating.value | append: '' | strip
      assign fq_ufh = fq.suitable_for_underfloor_heating.value | append: '' | strip | downcase
      assign fq_rect = fq.rectified.value | append: '' | downcase
      assign fq_mat = fq.tile_material.value | append: '' | downcase
    -%}
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Can I use this tile on the floor?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">{% if fq_s contains 'floor' or fq_s contains 'both' %}Yes, it's made for floors and walls.{% if fq_slip != '' %} Slip rating: {{ fq.slip_rating.value }}.{% endif %} In showers and wet rooms a matt or anti-slip finish is safer underfoot than polished.{% elsif fq_s contains 'wall' %}No, this is a wall tile. For floors, choose a tile rated for floor use.{% else %}Floors need a tile rated for floor use. Check "Use on" under Help choosing, or ask us.{% endif %}</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Is it suitable for underfloor heating?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">{% if fq_ufh contains 'yes' %}Yes. Use a flexible adhesive and let the heating come up to temperature slowly the first time.{% elsif fq_ufh contains 'no' %}We don't recommend this tile over underfloor heating.{% else %}Porcelain and ceramic tiles work well over underfloor heating with a flexible adhesive. Ask us if you'd like this one confirmed.{% endif %}</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>How many tiles do I need?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Measure the length and width of each area, multiply, and add the areas together. Add 10% for cuts and breakage, or 15% for herringbone and diagonal layouts. Type your area into the calculator above and it does the maths for you.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Which adhesive and grout should I use?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">{% if fq_mat contains 'porcelain' %}Porcelain needs a flexible cement-based adhesive (S1).{% else %}Use a cement-based tile adhesive; a flexible one (S1) is best on floors and over boards.{% endif %} Large tiles need a thicker, solid bed. The tiling kit above picks the right products and works out how much you need.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>What does rectified mean?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Rectified tiles are cut to exact size after firing, so they can be laid with slim 2mm joints for a near-seamless look. {% if fq_rect contains 'yes' %}This tile is rectified.{% elsif fq_rect contains 'no' %}This tile isn't rectified, so allow a 3mm joint.{% endif %}</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Do I need to seal these tiles?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">{% if fq_mat contains 'porcelain' or fq_mat contains 'ceramic' %}No. Glazed porcelain and ceramic tiles don't need sealing. A grout sealer helps keep light grout clean.{% else %}Porcelain and glazed ceramic don't need sealing. Natural stone and some unglazed tiles should be sealed before and after grouting.{% endif %}</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Will the colour match the photos?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Screens show colours differently, so order a sample to see it in your own light. Tiles can vary slightly from batch to batch, so order the full amount in one go.</div></details>
  {%- when 'taps' -%}
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Will this tap work on low water pressure?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Check the working pressure in the Specification tab or description. Combi boilers and unvented cylinders usually give plenty of pressure. Gravity-fed systems (a cold tank in the loft) often give less than 1 bar, so choose a low-pressure tap or fit a pump. Not sure? Send us your boiler or system type and we'll check.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>How many tap holes do I need?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Mono mixers need one tap hole, pillar taps need two and 3-hole mixers need three. Wall-mounted taps need none in the basin or bath. Check your basin or bath before ordering.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Is a waste included?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">If a waste is included it's listed in the description. If not, choose a slotted waste for a basin with an overflow hole, or an unslotted waste for a basin without one.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Can I fit it myself?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Swapping a deck-mounted tap like for like is a common DIY job if you're comfortable isolating the water. Wall-mounted taps need pipework inside the wall and are best fitted by a plumber.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Will it match my other brassware?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Choose the same finish name, for example brushed brass, matt black or chrome. Pieces from the same range are made to match; see Complete the look below.</div></details>
  {%- when 'kitchen-taps' -%}
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Will it fit my sink?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Most kitchen taps fit a standard single tap hole of around 35mm. Check the connection size in the Specification tab and the tap hole on your sink or worktop.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Will it work on low water pressure?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Check the working pressure in the Specification tab. Combi boilers and unvented cylinders usually give plenty of pressure; gravity-fed systems often give less than 1 bar. Send us your system type and we'll confirm.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Is a sink waste included?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Kitchen taps don't include a sink waste unless the description says so.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>How do I clean the finish?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Wipe with a soft damp cloth and dry off. Avoid abrasive pads, limescale removers and bleach, which can damage brushed and coloured finishes.</div></details>
  {%- when 'boiling-taps' -%}
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Is it safe with children?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Boiling taps have a child-safe control that needs two separate actions, such as push and turn, before boiling water flows. See the Specification tab for this model's safety control.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Do I need a separate tank?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">The tap heats water in a tank that sits under the sink. The description says whether the tank is included; if it isn't, choose the matching tank size.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>What do I need under the sink?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Cupboard space for the tank, a 13A socket and a cold water supply. Tank dimensions are in the Specification tab.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>How often do I change the filter?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">It depends on your water hardness and how much you use the tap. The filter life is listed in the Specification tab; hard water areas need more frequent changes.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Can I fit it myself?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">We recommend a qualified plumber. The tank needs a cold supply and a 13A socket, and it must be fitted to the manufacturer's instructions.</div></details>
  {%- when 'toilets' -%}
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Will it fit my existing soil pipe?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Check the outlet type and the distance from the wall to the centre of your soil pipe. Horizontal outlets suit most UK homes, and a pan connector adapts them to a soil pipe in the floor. Projection and dimensions are in the description.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>What does projection mean?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Projection is how far the toilet sticks out from the wall. Short projection toilets suit small bathrooms, ensuites and cloakrooms.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>What is comfort height?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">A comfort height pan sits higher than a standard toilet, so it's easier to sit down and stand up.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Is the seat included?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Yes where the title says the toilet comes with a seat. The seat type is shown in the description.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Does a wall hung toilet need a frame?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Yes. A wall hung pan needs a concealed cistern and frame fixed inside a stud or false wall, plus a flush plate. These are sold separately.</div></details>
  {%- when 'shower' -%}
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Will this work with my combi boiler?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Thermostatic showers work well with combi boilers and unvented cylinders, as long as your pressure is within the valve's working range shown in the Specification tab.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>I have a tank in the loft. Will it work?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Gravity-fed systems usually give low pressure, so check the minimum water pressure. You may need a shower pump; the right pump depends on where your tank sits, so message us and we'll advise.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>What are inlet centres?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">The distance between the hot and cold inlets on an exposed bar valve. The UK standard is 150mm, and the supplied elbows allow a little adjustment.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Which side is hot?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">UK convention is hot on the left and cold on the right. Check the fitting instructions before connecting.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Exposed or concealed?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Exposed valves fix to the wall surface, which suits renovations. Concealed valves sit inside the wall for a cleaner look but need the wall opening up, so they're best fitted before tiling.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Do I need a plumber?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">We recommend a qualified plumber for all shower installations.</div></details>
  {%- when 'mirrors' -%}
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Which bathroom zone can I fit it in?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">An IP44 rating covers Zone 2 and outside zones, typically above a basin. For directly above a bath or shower (Zone 1) an IP65 fitting is recommended.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Do I need an electrician?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Hardwired mirrors should be fitted by a qualified electrician on an RCD-protected circuit, in line with Part P Building Regulations.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Does the demister keep the mirror clear?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">The heated pad behind the glass keeps a central area clear after a hot shower. Give it a minute or two after switching on.</div></details>
  {%- when 'radiator-v3' -%}
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>What does BTU mean?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">BTU (British Thermal Units) is the heat output. The higher the figure, the more heat it gives. Use the calculator under Help choosing to see what your room needs. Outputs are quoted at ΔT50.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>How much heat do I need?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">As a rough guide allow around 100W per square metre of floor in an average room, and more for older properties, high ceilings, large windows or north-facing rooms. Outputs are quoted at ΔT50.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Are valves included?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Radiators are supplied without valves unless the description says otherwise. Add a pair to suit your pipework.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Will it work with a heat pump?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Yes, but heat pumps run at lower water temperatures, so a radiator gives out roughly half its ΔT50 figure. Size up accordingly.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>What are pipe centres?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">The distance between the two connections. Measure between your pipes and compare it with the pipe centres, allowing for the valves.</div></details>
  {%- when 'towel-rails' -%}
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>What does BTU mean?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">BTU (British Thermal Units) is the heat output. The higher the figure, the more heat it gives. Use the calculator under Help choosing to see what your room needs. Outputs are quoted at ΔT50.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Which valves do I need?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Match the valve to how your pipe meets the rail: pipe and inlet in a straight line needs a straight valve, a pipe coming up from the floor into a side inlet needs an angled valve, and a pipe from the wall into a side inlet needs a corner valve. Valves aren't included unless stated.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Can I use it in summer?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">A central-heating-only rail warms up only when your heating is on. A dual fuel rail with an electric element can run on its own. Check the heating type in the Specification tab, as not every rail can be converted.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>What size rail do I need?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Check the heat output in the Specification tab. As a towel warmer any size works; if the rail is the room's only heat source, make sure the output covers the room, or message us and we'll help.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Will it match my brushed brass taps?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Brushed gold and brushed brass are the same warm satin family and coordinate well. See Complete the look for pieces in the same finish.</div></details>
  {%- when 'radiator-valves' -%}
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Which valve do I need?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Pipe and radiator inlet in a straight line: straight valve. Pipe coming up from the floor into a side inlet: angled valve. Pipe coming out of the wall into a side inlet: corner valve.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Do valves come as a pair?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">The listing says whether it's a pair or a single valve. A radiator needs two: one on the flow and one lockshield on the return.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Microbore or 15mm?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Most UK systems use 15mm copper pipe. Older systems may use 8mm or 10mm microbore, so measure your pipe and use reducers if needed.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Do I need a TRV on every radiator?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Thermostatic valves are recommended on most radiators. The room with your main thermostat usually has a manual valve instead.</div></details>
  {%- when 'wastes' -%}
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Slotted or unslotted?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">If your basin has an overflow hole, use a slotted waste so water can reach the overflow. If it has no overflow, use an unslotted waste.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>What is a click-clack waste?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Press the plug to close it and press again to open. No chain or lever needed.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Is a trap included?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Traps are sold separately unless the description says otherwise.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Will it fit my basin?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Basin wastes are usually 1 1/4" thread and bath wastes 1 1/2". Check the connection size in the Specification tab.</div></details>
  {%- when 'bath-accessories' -%}
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Screw or adhesive fix?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Screw fixing is the strongest. Adhesive fixing avoids drilling into tiles. The description shows which fixing is supplied.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Are fixings included?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Wall fixings are included unless stated. For plasterboard walls use suitable cavity fixings.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>Will it match my taps?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Choose the same finish name as your taps and shower. Pieces from the same range are made to match; see Complete the look below.</div></details>
    <details class="tp-faq__i"><summary>How do I clean it?</summary><div class="tp-faq__a">Wipe with a soft damp cloth and dry off. Avoid abrasive or acidic cleaners.</div></details>
{%- endcase -%}